 desc='The <b>Sekmeer</b> is a small pool on the Hondsrug near Gasselte and, like the Zwanemeer and countless other round hollows in this landscape, it is almost certainly a <b>pingo scar</b> from the last glaciation. What makes the Sekmeer interesting is the <b>gradient</b> visible over a short distance: from open water through a floating bog-moss fringe and wet cross-leaved heath to dry ling heath on the sand crests, and from there into the surrounding Scots pine wood. Such complete transitions have become rare \u2014 in most places one link in the sequence has been lost to drainage or nutrient enrichment. The pool is <b>nutrient-poor and weakly acid</b>, with cotton grass, beaked sedge and locally marsh St John\u2019s-wort on the shores. For amphibians and dragonflies it is an important breeding site: <b>moor frog, smooth newt, black darter and white-faced darter</b>. Management focuses on keeping the shore zone open and preventing encroachment by trees.',
